{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nPlan review certified epic `1386-5e7c-391d-4a74` and two of its children with zero blocking\nfindings even though acceptance criteria across them were mutually unsatisfiable. Every\ncontradiction surfaced later — mid-implementation or at the completion-verifier close gate —\nand each correction forced a full LLM re-review because amending a description invalidates the\nattestation as `stale-material`. Bug `creamy-cocksure-elkhound` records the evidence (seven\ninstances in four failure classes). Until this lands, every plan author and every agent claiming\na ticket keeps paying that late-detection cost.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo defects with two separate causes. They ship as one story because the second one's proving\ntests instantiate the container pass that the first one's registration also touches, so splitting\nthem would duplicate that fixture and force one of the two to re-review against the other's\nchange to `pass1.py`.\n\n**(1) A new 1-TURN criterion `ac-satisfiability` (facet `coherence`).** It owns a concern no\ncriterion owns today: whether a ticket's own acceptance criteria and its Scope / Out-of-Scope\ndeclarations can all hold at once. `COH` covers contradictions BETWEEN sections and explicitly\ndisclaims within-section ones (\"not within-section nitpicks (those belong to E1/E2)\"); `E1`\ncovers criterion-to-description mapping, terminology and duplicates; `F1` covers measurability.\nNone asks whether the commitments are jointly satisfiable.\n\nThe rubric is written on a generic primitive rather than the motivating epic's subject matter:\n*an acceptance criterion quantifies over a set — universally (\"no file under X matches P\") or by\ncardinality (\"the N items are migrated\") — and something else in the same description places a\nmember inside the excepted region.* It names three shapes of that primitive:\nuniversal-vs-carve-out (a Scope or Out-of-Scope clause exempts a member of the set an AC\nquantifies over); derived-artifact closure (an AC asserts a property of an artifact the plan\nsays is regenerated from a source that keeps carrying the negation); and snapshot cardinality\n(an AC pins a count measured at authoring time for a set the plan says is discovered or\nrecomputed at execution time).\n\nPass-2 already grades exactly this defect: `internal_conflict` is a live verifier axis\n(`src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/verify_models.py:255`) and a member of `_PLAN_SEVERITY_AXES`\n(`src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/decide.py:309`). So the gap is confined to Pass-1 finding, and no\nverifier or impact-model change is part of this work. The criterion ships `advisory` at the\n`0.95` default; choosing a blocking posture needs replay data this change does not have yet.\n\nRejected: widening `COH` instead. It needs no new routing entry, but it puts two concerns in one\nsingle-turn scan and erases the boundary the rubrics police out loud. A separate routed\ncriterion is also close to free: `registry.chunk_by_facet`\n(`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/registry.py:434`) facet-sorts the single-turn tier then slices it\ninto fixed-size chunks, so chunk count is `ceil(total / n)` and one added criterion changes the\ncall count only when the applicable population is already an exact multiple of `n`. Single-turn\nchunks are never shed by the per-plan budget cap, so it cannot be starved on a large ticket the\nway an AGENT-tier alternative could.\n\n**(2) Make `G3`'s child-coverage audit able to fire.** `G3` asks whether the union of children\ncovers each parent acceptance criterion, and `plan_review_container.md` instructs it to flag an\nabsence \"only if NO sibling in the roster covers it\". But the roster it is handed carries ids\nand titles only, which cannot discharge that burden, so the anti-false-positive rule correctly\nsilences the finding every time. Give the roster each child's acceptance-criteria items,\nextracted with `evaluate_plan_clarity(...).ac_items` (`src/rebar/_plan_clarity.py:96`) — the same\nhelper the DET floor uses — and say so in `G3`'s rubric.\n\n*Single-source the builder.* That roster is currently built by two independent copies of the same\nexpression: the production path at `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py:154` and the spot-eval\nharness at `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/fidelity_spot_eval.py:219`, which feeds it to\n`passes.pass1_container` at lines 251 and 260. Changing only the production copy would make the\nharness send title-only rosters while production sends AC-bearing ones — and that harness is\nprecisely what would generate the dogfooding data for `G3`'s deferred posture decision, so the\ndivergence would corrupt the evidence for the next step. Extract one\n`build_sibling_roster(children)` helper into `pass1.py` and call it from both sites. It goes in\n`pass1.py` because that module already owns roster construction and has ample room under the\n800-LOC cap; rejected putting it in `passes.py`, which at 781 lines is too close to the cap to\nabsorb a new helper.\n\n*Where the roster is sent.* The roster is byte-identical across every pairing of one review, but\nit currently sits in `instructions` AFTER the per-pairing `children_block`, so it can never be\npart of a stable prefix. Fattening it in place would re-send the whole child\nacceptance-criteria corpus once per pairing, growing with the square of the child count. Instead\nsend it once in the byte-stable prefix: in `passes.pass1_container`, build the system prompt as\n`_resolve_system(PASS_CONTAINER, parent_plan, cfg)` concatenated with a rendered roster block,\nand delete the roster from `instructions`. This is plain string concatenation on the already\nresolved system prompt — `_resolve_system` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py:276`) keeps its\ncurrent `(prompt_id, plan, cfg)` signature, `plan_review_container.md` gains no new template\nvariable, and the roster stays a runtime value. In `passes.py` this is a move, not an addition:\nthe roster leaves `instructions` as it joins the system prompt, holding that file flat against\nthe cap.\n\n*Count the roster in the prefix budget.* `parent_tokens` (`pass1.py:156`) is estimated from the\nparent plan alone, and it feeds three decisions: container bin-packing\n(`sizing.pack_container_bins`, `pass1.py:166`), the per-pairing size estimate (`pass1.py:168`),\nand the warm-then-fan-out gate `parent_tokens >= CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS` (`pass1.py:48`, `:187`).\nOnce the roster is part of the prefix, it must be included in that estimate or bins can be packed\nover the window budget. Add it to the estimate.\n\nNote the honest limit of the caching claim: sending the roster once in a byte-stable prefix makes\nit *cacheable* and stops it being re-sent per pairing, but whether a cache is actually written\nstill depends on the prefix clearing `CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS` (4096) and on there being at least\ntwo pairings. A larger prefix makes that gate more likely to pass, not less. The acceptance\ncriteria below therefore assert the structural property (the roster is sent in the prefix, once,\nnot repeated in per-pairing instructions), which is deterministic and testable, rather than a\ncache-hit rate.\n\nRejected for (2): making the new criterion agentic so it fetches siblings itself. Cross-child\ncontradiction is already `G4`'s declared concern, so that would duplicate an owned concern and\nforce AGENT cost onto the cheap check. `G3`'s posture and threshold are deliberately left\nuntouched — revisiting them needs dogfooding data from this change.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_ac_satisfiability.md` — new: the criterion rubric\n  (prompt id `plan-review-ac-satisfiability`, per the stem-underscores-to-dashes convention in\n  `prompts._packaged_prompt_files`).\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_G3.md` — rubric: discharge the sibling test against the\n  roster's acceptance criteria.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json` — the `ac-satisfiability` routing entry.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/registry.py` — add `ac-satisfiability` to `CANONICAL_LLM`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py` — the shared `build_sibling_roster` helper; include the\n  roster in the `parent_tokens` prefix estimate.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/fidelity_spot_eval.py` — call the shared helper instead of its own\n  copy of the roster expression.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py` — move the roster from `instructions` into the system\n  prompt.\n- `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` — regenerated from the registry.\n- `tests/unit/test_ac_satisfiability_criterion.py` — new.\n- `tests/unit/test_container_roster.py` — new.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: with `ac-satisfiability` registered, `validate_packaged_routing()` returns an\n  empty list and `chunk_by_facet` places it in the facet-sorted `coherence` group alongside\n  `COH` and `E1`.\n- Happy path: the roster built for a parent whose children carry `## Acceptance Criteria`\n  sections contains each child's criterion text.\n- Wiring, end to end through Pass-1: drive the single-turn chunk path with a `FakeRunner`\n  (`src/rebar/llm/runner.py:111`) and assert the new criterion reaches the finder rubric and that\n  a finding it emits survives criterion-attribution validation. This proves the criterion is\n  actually wired into a review rather than only registered; judging its LLM accuracy is what the\n  deferred dogfooding pass is for.\n- Edge: a child whose description has no `## Acceptance Criteria` section yields a roster entry\n  without raising.\n- Empty: a parent with no children yields an empty roster without raising.\n- Contract: both the production path and the spot-eval harness obtain their roster from the one\n  shared builder, so they cannot diverge.\n- Contract: the container pass sends the roster in its system prompt and not in its per-pairing\n  instructions.\n- Regression: the existing plan-review unit suite stays green.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `ac-satisfiability` is a registered plan-review criterion routed as `exec: \"1-TURN\"`,\n      `facet: \"coherence\"`, `default_posture: \"advisory\"`; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_ac_satisfiability_criterion.py::test_routing_entry` and by\n      `validate_packaged_routing()` returning an empty list.\n- [ ] Its rubric states the set-quantification primitive and names all three contradiction\n      shapes (universal-vs-carve-out, derived-artifact closure, snapshot cardinality), and does\n      not reference the `Success Criteria` migration that motivated it; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_ac_satisfiability_criterion.py::test_rubric_is_generic`.\n- [ ] The criterion is exercised end to end through the Pass-1 single-turn chunk path against a\n      `FakeRunner`, and a finding it emits survives attribution validation; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_ac_satisfiability_criterion.py::test_reaches_pass1_and_attributes`.\n- [ ] `rebar explain ac-satisfiability` prints the criterion's guide section; verified by\n      running that command and observing a non-empty section.\n- [ ] Nothing under `src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/` changes, because Pass-2's\n      `internal_conflict` axis already grades this defect; verified by\n      `git diff --exit-code origin/main -- src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/` exiting 0.\n- [ ] The sibling roster carries each child's acceptance-criteria items, not only ids and titles;\n      verified by `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_carries_child_ac`.\n- [ ] Both `pass1.py` and `fidelity_spot_eval.py` obtain the roster from the single shared\n      `build_sibling_roster` helper, and neither retains its own roster-building expression;\n      verified by `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_builder_single_sourced`.\n- [ ] A child description with no `## Acceptance Criteria` section, and a parent with no\n      children, each produce a roster without raising; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_degrades_gracefully`.\n- [ ] `G3`'s rubric instructs the reviewer to test \"no sibling covers it\" against the roster's\n      acceptance criteria; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_g3_rubric_cites_roster_ac`.\n- [ ] The container pass sends the roster inside its system prompt and not inside its\n      per-pairing instructions; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_rides_prefix_not_instructions`.\n- [ ] The roster's token cost is included in the `parent_tokens` prefix estimate that feeds\n      container bin-packing and the warm gate; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_counted_in_prefix_estimate`.\n- [ ] `G3`'s `default_posture` and `block_threshold` are unchanged from their pre-change values;\n      verified by `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_g3_posture_unchanged`.\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` parity-checks clean against the registry; verified by\n      `validate_criteria_guide()` returning an empty list.\n- [ ] No `src/rebar/**/*.py` file exceeds the 800-LOC cap; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py`.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and `pytest tests/unit` all pass."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785376787613926001,"uuid":"b8f7cd07-5f22-4631-8384-fbe0e68aad1c"}